---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import YouTube from '../components/YouTube.astro';
import Steam from '../components/Steam.astro';
import Video from '../components/Video.astro';
import Loop from '../components/Loop.astro';
import ProjectCard from '../components/ProjectCard.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Unity: Restitched | Linus Ekberg"
  description="Restitched by Trixel Creative: gameplay systems and editor tools for a community-driven 2.5D platformer."
>
  <div class="project wrap">
    <Card class="intro-card">
      <div class="cols top">
        <div class="stack">
          <div>
            <h1 class="page-title">Restitched - Trixel Creative</h1>
            <ul class="facts">
              <li>Team: 15-20 people</li>
              <li>Unity, C#, .NET</li>
              <li>Role: Programmer/Engineer</li>
              <li>In development (joined February 2025, ongoing)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="prose emph">
            <p>
              Restitched is a community-driven 2.5D platformer featuring robust level and costume creation tools. My role
              focuses on engineering scalable gameplay systems and editor tools designed for a networked multiplayer
              environment, ensuring that players can create, share, and play content seamlessly.
            </p>
            <p class="note">
              <em>
                As of now, I can only share what has already been published by Trixel Creative. Therefore, I am limited
                in what I can present here.
              </em>
            </p>
          </div>
          <Steam app={1361090} title="Restitched on Steam" />
        </div>
        <div class="stack trailers">
          <YouTube id="nlUnVR6NdT4" title="Restitched - Official Trailer" />
          <YouTube id="ZoXH1_Vgt-k" title="Restitched | Official Wardrobe Trailer" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Wardrobe">
      <p class="center emph">Restitched: Wardrobe Demo is out now to try!</p>
      <Steam app={4649960} title="Restitched: Wardrobe Demo on Steam" class="demo-widget" />
      <div class="wardrobe">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            I engineered tools for the character customization architecture for the <strong>Wardrobe Demo</strong>. This
            standalone release is designed to populate the <strong>Steam Workshop</strong> and stress-test our deployment
            pipelines. Importantly, all assets created &amp; uploaded during this phase are fully forward-compatible with
            the release of the main game's wardrobe launch.
          </p>
          <p>
            One of my focuses was the <strong>Stamping System</strong>, a feature allowing players to project and layer
            decals onto 3D character meshes. Ensuring precision and compatibility across hundreds of costume pieces, with
            virtually an infinite amount of combinations, presented unique technical challenges.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Pixel-Perfect Selection (GPU ID Buffers):
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <strong>The Challenge:</strong> Standard raycasting was insufficient for selecting specific stamps, especially
                  when they overlapped or had complex shapes. Initially, clicking a stamp often selected its square UV bounding
                  box rather than the visible pixels, making it impossible to click "through" the transparency of one stamp to
                  select another behind it.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>The Solution:</strong> I implemented a GPU-based selection system using Multiple Render Targets (MRT).
                  This allowed me to generate a unique ID map in parallel with the visual render without incurring the cost of a
                  second geometry pass. However, standard shaders drew the entire quad to the ID buffer. I modified the fragment
                  shader to apply a hard alpha clip to the ID output (discarding transparent pixels) while preserving smooth alpha
                  blending for the visual color output. This ensured click detection matched the visual shape perfectly.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="short">
          <YouTube id="7r0Hq0VEJoQ" title="Wardrobe Overview by Plush Piney" vertical />
          <figcaption>Wardrobe Overview by Plush Piney</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <ul class="prose continued">
        <li>
          Optimizing GPU Readbacks:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>The Challenge:</strong> To identify which stamp the user was hovering over, the system needed to read
              the pixel color from the ID texture on the GPU. Doing this every frame (Texture2D.ReadPixels) would cause
              significant CPU-GPU synchronization stalls, killing performance.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The Solution:</strong> I implemented texture caching. Instead of reading from the GPU every frame, the
              system pulls a readable copy of the texture to the CPU only when the user switches cosmetic targets. Subsequent
              hover checks are done via a fast array lookup on the CPU. I further optimized this by throttling the detection
              logic to run only when necessary (e.g., on stamping changes, mouse movement), rather than inside the Update loop.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
          Data Integrity &amp; Serialization:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>The Challenge:</strong> Managing the lifecycle of stamps (adding, removing, re-indexing) often led to
              desynchronization between the visual render and the underlying data structure, particularly when stamps were
              deleted from the middle of the stack.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The Solution:</strong> I designed a robust management system that ensures the "Runtime ID" (used for GPU
              picking) always maps correctly to the stamp's index in the underlying data collection, auto-correcting IDs
              whenever the stack is modified or loaded from a save file.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Level Editor Architecture">
      <div class="prose">
        <!-- Keep inline tags off line edges: Astro drops a line break that touches a tag. -->
        <p>
          During my work on Restitched, I have focused on the level creation tools and gameplay systems, with an <strong>emphasis</strong> on <strong>stability</strong> and <strong>network compatibility</strong>.
          Modularity is key, since Restitched is made of a wide variety of placeable objects, the <strong>tools</strong> must be <strong>flexible</strong>, <strong>easy to use</strong> and work <strong>reliably</strong> in a <strong>networked environment</strong>.
          To support this, I designed datasets where designers can easily create new data with unique properties. For
          example, I implemented a lethal dataset that allows designers to easily define lethals (property that makes objects
          dangerous). Once defined, these automatically appear in the level editor, giving players immediate access without
          additional engineering work.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cols media-pair">
        <figure>
          <Video name="dev-video-lethal-tool-shaders-progress-134264553" width={449} class="shadow" label="Applying lethality to an object" />
          <figcaption>Video of applying lethality to object.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Loop name="copy-lethals" width={449} label="Copying the lethal type between objects" />
          <figcaption>Shortcut to copy and paste the lethal type just by clicking on other objects.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I'm currently developing the joint and connector system for Restitched's level creator. These tools allow players
          to link objects together with physics-driven behaviors such as pistons, strings, bolts, and motors. The system is
          designed to support a wide variety of connectors within a shared framework, making it straightforward to expand
          with new types in the future.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since Restitched supports multiplayer creation, an additional challenge is ensuring physics-based interactions
          remain deterministic, stable and synchronized across the network. The goal is to provide designers and players
          with responsive and reliable building blocks that enable the creation of complex contraptions and mechanics.
          Joints and connectors aim is to make levels feel more alive as they interact and manipulate physics. This feature
          is still in development and has not yet been showcased publicly.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="feature">
        <YouTube id="qdFmEQhdZ_Y" title="Restitched | Lethal Tool Feature" />
        <figcaption>Lethal tool that I worked on</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </Card>

    <Card class="more">
      <p>Wanna know more about my projects? Read more here:</p>
      <ProjectCard href="/brewbesties" image="maincapsule-bb.png" width={400} ratio="400 / 216" alt="Brew Besties" />
    </Card>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Classes passed to the Card component land on its own element, so they need :global. */
  :global(.intro-card) {
    padding: 18px 34px 32px 23px;
  }

  .top {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 500px) minmax(0, 401px);
    justify-content: space-between;
  }

  .facts {
    margin-top: 6px;
  }

  .prose {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 0 !important;
  }

  .trailers {
    gap: 48px;
    padding-top: 22px;
  }

  :global(.demo-widget) {
    margin: 6px auto 30px;
  }

  .wardrobe {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 580px) 236px;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 40px;
  }

  .short {
    padding-top: 10px;
  }

  .prose ul,
  .continued {
    margin-top: 12px;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .continued {
    padding-left: 1.4em;
  }

  .continued ul {
    list-style: circle;
  }

  .media-pair {
    margin: 30px 0 30px;
  }

  .feature {
    width: min(480px, 100%);
    margin: 30px auto 0;
  }

  :global(.more) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    justify-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    padding-top: 18px;
    padding-bottom: 12px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    :global(.intro-card) {
      padding: 16px 18px 26px;
    }

    .top,
    .wardrobe {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .short {
      width: min(280px, 100%);
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
